      <profileDesc>
         <abstract xml:id="about">
            <p>The Alia musica was not conceived by a single author at a single time. Instead, it is the result of a
               layering of commentaries by successive authors (<persName ref="#beta">β</persName>, <persName ref="#gamma">γ</persName> and <persName ref="#delta">δ</persName>) upon a core treatise (<persName ref="#alpha">Author α</persName>). The text-historical edition represents this process of commenting
               in its chronological order, as outlined by Charles M. Atkinson, The Critical Nexus: Tone-System, Mode,
               and Notation in Early Medieval Music (Oxford, New York: Oxford University Press, 2009): 171-201. This
               edition does not represent a specific manuscript or an archetype of transmission, but is intended to
               display the stages of commentary in chronological order. Each stage in the genesis of the text can be
               accessed separately.</p>
            <p>
               <persName ref="#alpha">Author δ</persName> played a particularly important role in the creation of the
               final text. He acted not only as a commentator, but also structured the treatise as we know it from the
               manuscript transmission by adding headings and changing the order of the material, placing most of the
               core treatise, <persName ref="#alpha">α</persName>, at the end of the work. A comparison between the
               genealogical order of commenting and the final arrangement, as represented in the manuscripts, is
               provided by the overview.</p>
         </abstract>
         <abstract xml:id="edition-a">
            <p>This is the core treatise for the Alia musica, Atkinson’s <persName ref="#alpha">Author α</persName>,
               dating from the late 9th century. It presents 8 toni, or church modes, describing each both verbally and
               mathematically.</p>
         </abstract>
         <abstract xml:id="edition-ab">
            <p>This presents the core treatise by <persName ref="#alpha">Author α</persName> as framed and commented
               upon by a second author, Atkinson´s <persName ref="#beta">Author β</persName> (early 10th c.).</p>
         </abstract>
         <abstract xml:id="edition-abg">
            <p>This presents the core treatise (<persName ref="#alpha">Author α</persName>), its frame and commentary
                  (<persName ref="#beta">Author β</persName>), and a tonary, the Nova expositio (<persName ref="#gamma">Author γ</persName>), transmitted independently, but added to the first two layers (later 10th
               c.).</p>
         </abstract>
         <abstract xml:id="edition-abgd">
            <p>This presents the sum of the core treatise and its commentaries. All parts of the text are located in the
               order by which the commentary grew before <persName ref="#delta">Author δ</persName>’s final redaction,
               which shifted <persName ref="#alpha">Author α</persName>’s work to the end of the treatise, where we find
               it in the manuscripts of the complete work.</p>
         </abstract>
         <abstract xml:id="overview">
            <p>This presents the organization of the entire treatise before and after <persName ref="#delta">Author
                  δ</persName>’s rearrangement of the order of materials. „α + β + γ + δ“ shows the sum of the core
               treatise and its commentaries, „Manuscript Order“ shows the arrangement of the text as we find it in the
               manuscripts (e.g. Munich, Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, clm 14272, and Paris, Bibliothèque nationale de
               France, lat. 7211) – the arrangement that <persName ref="#delta">Author δ</persName> created when he
               shifted from commenting to redacting the final version of the treatise.</p>
         </abstract>
